There are several ways to center a div horizontally in CSS. Here are the most common and effective methods:

## 1. **Flexbox (Recommended)**

```css
.container {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

```html
<div class="container">
  <div class="centered-div">This div is centered</div>
</div>
```

## 2. **Text-align (for inline/inline-block elements)**

```css
.container {
  text-align: center;
}

.centered-div {
  display: inline-block;
}
```

## 3. **Margin auto (for block elements with width)**

```css
.centered-div {
  width: 300px; /* Must have a width */
  margin: 0 auto;
}
```

## 4. **CSS Grid**

```css
.container {
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  /* or */
  justify-content: center;
}
```

## 5. **Absolute positioning with transform (for fixed positioning)**

```css
.centered-div {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  transform: translateX(-50%);
}
```

## Best Practice
**Use Flexbox** (`display: flex; justify-content: center;`) as it's modern, flexible, and widely supported. It's the most reliable method for horizontal centering.

Choose the method that best fits your layout structure and browser support requirements.

Use `margin: 0 auto;` on the div, *but only if it has a defined width*.  

If the div is `width: 100%`, it will already stretch to the edges, so `margin: 0 auto` won’t work. Instead, use `display: flex` on the parent (or `text-align: center` if it’s inline).

**Best solution for modern layouts:**
```css
.parent {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

The `margin: 0 auto` method is outdated and unreliable in many contexts. Use flexbox — it’s clean, predictable, and works in all modern browsers.
